     "derivation": "NPers. هزار hazār 'thousand' continues Middle Persian hazār (Book Pahlavi written with the arameogram-like spelling ⟨hcʾl⟩, Manichaean hz'r; MacKenzie, CPD 43), from Old Iranian *hazahra- = Av. hazaŋra-. The Middle Persian outcome of Old Iranian *-zahr-/-zaŋr- is -zār-, whence the New Persian form unchanged since the Sasanian period; Armenian borrowed it as hazar already in the Parthian/Sasanian period (Hübschmann, Arm. Gr. I 174), and the Sogdian cognate is z'r. Note that Nourai draws the arrow Avestan→Persian; strictly the Persian word is not descended from Avestan but from the parallel Old Persian/Old Iranian form, Avestan simply being the attested Old Iranian witness — a shorthand Nourai uses throughout the book.",
